Ownership, governance and a five-year handover plan for a third-generation manufacturer.

The founder's grandchildren were now shareholders, but only two of them worked in the company. The founder still made most decisions, and the board met only when there was a crisis. Everyone agreed that a change was needed, but nobody agreed on how to start.

We began with private conversations. Each family member described what they wanted from the company, and what they were prepared to give. The differences were large, and we did not try to hide them. We wrote the results on one page, which became the basis for a family charter.

Next, we designed the governance. The company got an independent board member, a clear split between family and management roles, and a yearly review of the handover plan. The founder agreed to step back from daily decisions over five years, with a fixed set of milestones.

The plan includes training for the next generation, a rotation through three departments and a written list of the skills each role needs. The first milestone was met on time, and the family now meets four times a year with an agenda that everyone helped to write.
